          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I feel love for you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The longer turn intensifies this avowal by claiming that her love exceeds words and other goods.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows a present state but does not itself promise future conduct, assume a new obligation, or renew a specific undertaking. Lear's subsequent grant is his response to the avowal, not a commitment made by Goneril through these words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"This is a composed answer to Lear's formal request in a public competition, not a reflex-like affective outburst. Its emotional content and rhetorical intensity do not establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Our eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression functions strategically as Goneril's competitive answer in Lear's love test, intended to qualify her for his bounty. This courtly, inheritance-seeking function is not fully captured by T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Our eldest born, speak first.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Goneril addresses her father, King Lear, in a public and explicitly competitive love test. Lear has asked which daughter loves him most and has tied the answers to the distribution of his kingdom. The target begins Goneril's elaborate profession of surpassing filial love, after which Lear awards her a portion.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"absent\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril makes no specific undertaking in the focal words. Lear nevertheless accepts the profession as satisfying his test and responds by granting her and Albany's line a territorial share. That reward is uptake of her avowal and bid, not uptake of a promise. Later supplied evidence—including Kent's description of the sisters as “empty-hearted” and Goneril's hostile treatment of Lear—supports reading the profession as insincere, though insincerity does not reduce its strong T force.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear, and the focal expression is from Goneril's speech in the opening love test.\",\"limitations\":\"The exact target is only the opening clause of a longer speech. The supplied text strongly supports a strategic and insincere reading, but it does not provide direct access to Goneril's private mental state at the instant of speaking; the affection judgment therefore rests on her represented conduct and other characters' assessments.\"}"
